  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nAn upper level wave within a building upper level ridge is departing \neastward early this afternoon. Lingering moisture and daytime \nheating will result in isolated showers and thunderstorms this \nafternoon and early evening, mainly near terrain. The overall \nmagnitude of instability is low however, which limits any concerns\nfor a stronger thunderstorm today. \n\nA more anti-cyclonic flow develops into Thursday, which will \nsuppress any shower or thunderstorm activity ahead of an approaching \nupper level trough that moves overhead Friday. Gusty southerly to \nsouthwesterly winds look to develop just ahead of the passage of the \ntrough and associated cold front Friday. A gusty wind shift looks to \naccompany the front, with a few thunderstorms developing along it \nacting to locally amplify wind gusts. Most areas look to see winds \nat least briefly subside after frontal passage Friday night, though \nalong the Rocky Mountain Front and adjacent plains strong cross \nbarrier flow and ridgetop stability will keep gusty winds going \nthrough the night into Saturday. \n\nAs daytime mixing ensues Saturday, gusty winds become more common \nagain, particularly across the plains given respectable mid level \nflow continuing across the region. Another trough looks to move \nonshore across the west late weekend into early next week, though \nconfidence in its progression and whether or not there is an \nadditional reinforcing trough remains low. The main takeaway for \nSunday into early next week will be for near to slightly below \naverage temperatures, with low-end probabilities for precipitation. \n-AM\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nThe main source of uncertainty which will help determine how \nimpactful Friday afternoon and evening will be is related to timing \nof the cold front. Most guidance has settled on late afternoon and \nearly evening, which would be the most favorable timing for \nconvective enhancement of wind gusts near any shower or thunderstorm \nthat develops. Should this trend much later, the risk for convective \nenhancement would diminish somewhat.\n\nThat said, cooler air moving in near the surface will still result \nin a favorable mountain wave setup near the Rocky Mountain Front \nand adjacent plains, which will result in a stretch of gusty winds\ninto Saturday. The most favored timeframe for a strong wind gust \nis Friday evening into early Friday night, which is when cross \nbarrier flow is strongest. The probability for a 58+ mph gust \naround Cut Bank is roughly 50-60%. Given this is still a bit more \nthan 48 hours away, no High Wind Watches are being considered at \nthis time. -AM",
